course: language
lesson: 5
title: Pronouns: I, me, you, they and who
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A pronoun stands in for a noun so you don't repeat it: "Mia went to the shop. Mia bought milk. Mia went home" becomes "Mia went to the shop. She bought milk and went home."
explain: Pronouns come in two forms: subject forms do the action (I, he, she, we, they) and object forms receive it (me, him, her, us, them); "Sam and I went" but "Mum gave sweets to Ben and me".
explain: The take-out trick: remove the other person and listen; "Mum gave sweets to I" sounds wrong and "to me" sounds right, so it is "to Ben and me".
explain: Standard English has no special plural "you", so dialects made their own: youse in Ireland, Liverpool and Glasgow, y'all in the southern US, yinz in Pittsburgh; long ago English had "thou" for one friend and "you" for many or for someone you respected.
explain: A pronoun must point clearly: in "Max told Leo that he had won" nobody knows who won, so a good writer uses a name again.
fact: A pronoun is a word that takes the place of a noun, like I, you, he, she, it, we and they.
fact: Subject pronouns are I, he, she, we, they; object pronouns are me, him, her, us, them.
fact: In standard written English the subject is "Sam and I went"; "me and Sam went" is very common in everyday speech and everyone understands it.
fact: After a verb or a word like "to" or "with", the object form is needed: "to Ben and me", "with them".
fact: Putting the other person first, as in "Sam and I", is a polite habit.
fact: Youse means you, more than one person, in Ireland, Liverpool, Glasgow, parts of Australia and elsewhere; yinz means you all in Pittsburgh in the US.
fact: Long ago "thou" was the friendly word for one person you knew well, like Ukrainian ти or French tu, and "you" or "ye" was for more than one person or someone you respected.
fact: In parts of Yorkshire and the North some older speakers still say "tha" for you and "thine" for yours; these come from the old thou and thee.
fact: Singular "they" for one person whose gender is unknown has been used since the 1300s; some people today also use it as their own pronoun, and major dictionaries accept both uses.
fact: Young children often say "him did it" or "me want it"; this is a normal stage, and they grow out of it by hearing the grown-up forms.
fact: Whom is the old object form of who, like him for he; it is now mostly used in formal writing, especially after a word like "to" ("to whom").
fact: The antecedent is the noun a pronoun points back to; a pronoun is ambiguous when it could point to more than one person.
exercise: Mia writes "Mia went to the shop. Mia bought milk." What would make it better? | Use a pronoun: "She bought milk." | Repeat the name even more, so nobody gets confused | Leave out the second sentence | Use "it" for Mia
exercise: What is a pronoun? | A word that takes the place of a noun | A word that describes a verb, like quickly or softly | A word for a group of animals | A word that joins two sentences
exercise: Jack says "Me and Sam went swimming." What would a formal piece of writing say? | Sam and I went swimming | Sam and me went swimming | I and Sam went swimming | Me and Sam goes swimming
exercise: Lola writes "Mum gave sweets to Ben and I." What should it be? | to Ben and me | to Ben and myself | to I and Ben | to Ben and he
exercise: What is the take-out trick? | Remove the other person and see which pronoun sounds right alone | Remove all the pronouns and use everybody's names instead | Take out the verb and read it again | Take out the last word of the sentence
exercise: Which of these are all object pronouns? | me, him, us | I, he, we | she, they, I | we, you, he
exercise: Oisin's nan shouts "Youse two, get in here!" What does youse mean? | You, more than one person | You, one person, politely | Use the two of them | Yours, belonging to you
exercise: Why did dialects make words like youse and y'all? | Standard English has no separate plural "you" | People there forgot how to say "you" the normal way | They are spelling mistakes from texting | Youse is the Latin word for you
exercise: In Shakespeare, what was "thou"? | The friendly word for one person you knew well | The poshest, most formal word, used only for strangers | The word for a king only | A word for more than one person
exercise: A dad from Yorkshire says "Is that thine?" What is he asking? | Is that yours? | Is that mine? | Is that thin? | Is that fine?
exercise: Hamid asks why "Someone left their coat" uses "their" for one person. What is true? | Singular they has been used for an unknown person since the 1300s | It is a mistake that was invented recently by texting kids | Their always means two people or more | It should be "theirs coat"
exercise: Ana's little brother shouts "Him did it!" What's going on? | It's a normal stage of learning, and he'll grow out of it | He will speak badly forever unless someone tells him off | He is speaking a different language | He has copied a grown-up mistake
exercise: What is "whom"? | The old object form of who, now mostly formal | A spelling mistake for who, made in old printed books | The plural of who | A word meaning home
exercise: Tilly writes "Max told Leo that he had won." What is the problem? | "He" could mean Max or Leo | "He" must always mean Leo | "Told" is the wrong verb | There is no problem at all
exercise: How can Tilly fix it? | Use a name: "Max told Leo that Leo had won." | Change "he" to "it" | Add more pronouns | Swap "told" for "said"
exercise: In "Tom's sister lent him her pen because he had lost his", whose pen does Tom now hold? | His sister's | His own lost pen | Nobody's, it is a new pen | It is impossible to tell at all
exercise: How many pronouns are in "She told me that they would meet us there"? | Four: she, me, they, us | Three: she, me, us | Five: she, me, that, they, us | Two: she and they
exercise: Which is correct after "between"? | between you and me | between you and I | between I and you | between yous and I
test: Your mate goes "and I is always the posh correct one, innit." Which sentence proves him wrong? | Gran sent a card to Ben and me | Ben and I went home | Me and Ben went home | Ben and me went home
test: A girl from Glasgow says "Youse are pure dead brilliant!" ("Pure dead" means really.) Who is she talking to? | More than one person | One person only | Herself | Nobody, she means "use"
test: A kid from Pittsburgh asks "Are yinz coming to the game?" What does he mean? | Are you all coming? | Are the twins coming? | Are you winning the game? | Are your friends coming in?
test: Your cousin reckons "thou" was always the posh, formal word. What's true? | It was the friendly, informal word for one person | He's right, kings and queens used it only to each other | It was the plural, like "you all" | It was never a real English word
test: Grandad says "singular they is a brand-new invention, no cap." What is the fact? | It has been used for one unknown person since the 1300s | It was invented by a dictionary in 2020 for modern writing | It was invented by a dictionary in 2020 | It is only used in American films
test: What would happen if English had no pronouns at all? | We'd repeat names and nouns again and again | Nothing, because names already do the same job perfectly | Sentences would become shorter | We'd never know who did anything
test: A boomer teacher says "To whom it may concern" is the only way to start every letter. Do you need "whom" in a note to your mate? | No, in everyday speech "who" is fine | Yes, "whom" is needed in every sentence | Yes, or the letter is illegal | No, use "whomst" instead
test: In a cartoon, a robot says "Me want battery!" and the kids laugh. What is the grown-up form? | I want a battery | Me wants a battery | Myself want battery | Him want battery
test: Tilly reads "Jo phoned Kim after she got home." Her friend asks who got home. What is the honest answer? | It's ambiguous; it could be Jo or Kim | It must be Kim, the nearest name | It must be Jo, the first name | It's nobody, "she" has no meaning
test: A Yorkshire grandad says "Where's tha going, lad?" What does he mean? | Where are you going? | Where is that going? | Where is the lad going? | Where are they going?
test: Lola's pal writes "The teacher smiled at Sam and I." Using the take-out trick, what should it be? | at Sam and me, because "smiled at me" | at Sam and I, because "and I" is polite | at I and Sam, because I comes first | at Sam and myself, because it's posher
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a pronoun? | A word employed in place of a noun so as to avoid repetition | A word qualifying the action denoted by a verb in a clause | A word denoting a collection of animals | A word conjoining two clauses
exam: Ascertain the correctly formulated sentence in formal written English. | My mother gave sweets to Ben and me | My mother gave sweets to Ben and I | My mother gave sweets to I and Ben | My mother gave sweets to Ben and myself
exam: Identify the principal reason for the emergence of dialect forms such as "youse" and "y'all". | Standard English possesses no distinct plural form of the second-person pronoun | Speakers of those dialects are unacquainted with the word "you" | Such forms derive from errors in printed dictionaries | Such forms were imposed by the authorities of those regions
exam: In respect of the sentence "Max told Leo that he had won", select the response consistent with sound reasoning. | The pronoun is ambiguous, as it may refer to either person | The pronoun necessarily refers to Max | The pronoun necessarily refers to Leo | The pronoun refers to neither person
